  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 342 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nStratus remained in place over much of the Northland this \nafternoon in response to a broad area of isentropic ascent ahead\nof an approaching shortwave trough. Thunderstorms percolated \nalong the Canadian border west of Baudette this morning and \nrecently began to intensify. Will keep an eye on these storms as\nthe propagate eastward into north-central Minnesota. The \nstratus limited temperatures to the upper 60s to middle 70s for \nmost locations today which likely has negative effect on \ninstability at the moment. Skies are clearing south of the area \nof storms and rapid heating may develop as the clearing moves \ninto western Itasca and western Koochiching counties.\n\nHigh-res guidance has been of little use so far today as the \nmodels are under-representing the ongoing convection. They have \nbeen consistent in developing another round of strong \nthunderstorms over far northeast North Dakota and northwest \nMinnesota between 22Z and 25.00Z and propagating them eastward \ninto north-central Minnesota. Recent regional radar mosaic \nrevealed strengthening storms along and north of an outflow \nboundary from earlier convection. Southerly low-level flow is \namplified in this area per the latest SPC RAP mesoanalysis. \nThere is divergence at 850 mb in that area with stronger \nsouthwesterly flow over northwest Ontario and strong convergence\nat 700 mb. These factors will continue to support storm \nredevelopment through early evening. Hodographs reveal favorable\nveering in the 0-6 km layer for storm-scale organization with a\nlong, straight hodograph above ~4 km, which will support \nefficient mass evacuation. Look for embedded supercells \ninitially with the potential for large hail and perhaps a \ntornado this afternoon into early evening. Cold pool \nconsolidation will lead to upscale growth and a transition to a \nhail and wind threat.\n\nWe will need to be mindful of training storms and the potential\nfor excessive rainfall. KMVX storm total precip from 1205Z \nuntil 2007Z reveals a narrow stripe of more than 3 inches of \nrain.\n\nHeat and humidity return starting Saturday and will persist \nthrough early next week. We issued a Heat Advisory for Saturday \nover most of the Northland and an Extreme Heat Watch for Sunday.\nAdditional heat headlines may be needed Monday and Tuesday.\n\nIn addition to the heat Saturday, there is a risk of a storm or\ntwo breaking through the cap. In the event storms develop, they\nwill be capable of large hail and damaging wind. As of this \nafternoon, the risk of storms is less than 10% for any given \nlocation.\n\nSunday will bring another potential for showers and storms, \nsome of which may be severe. An elevated mixed layer is forecast\nto advect eastward over the region which will limit storm \npotential initially. Strong heating beneath the cap will result \nin an area of 3000-4000 J/kg of MUCAPE. There is a small chance \na few storms may break through the cap in the afternoon. The \nmore likely evolution will be storms developing near a cold \nfront in southern Manitoba and northeast North Dakota and moving\ninto north-central and central Minnesota during the evening. If\nstorms form in the afternoon, very large hail and damaging \nwinds are possible. With the later round of storms, damaging \nwinds will be the main threat.\n\nTemperatures cool a bit behind the front Monday. Heat headlines\nmay be needed once again. For the remainder of next week, \ntemperatures will be in the 80s and low 90s until Friday. \nAdditional heat headlines may be needed. There will also be \nperiodic chances of rain and storms, mainly focused Wednesday \nnight and beyond.",
